<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.17. The Fourier transform intro</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The Fourier Transform is an incredibly powerful tool that can help use solve PDEs.
      Some properties first...
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <fact xml:id="fact-eulers-formula">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Euler's Formula</term> states (some rearrangment)
        <md>
          <mrow> e^{i\theta} = \cos(\theta)+i\sin(\theta), ~~~~~ \amp ~~~~~ \cos(\theta) = \frac12\left(e^{i\theta}+e^{-i\theta}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow> e^{-i\theta} = \cos(\theta)-i\sin(\theta), ~~~~~ \amp ~~~~~ \sin(\theta) = \frac1{2i}\left(e^{i\theta}-e^{-i\theta}\right)</mrow>
        </md>        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </fact>

<definition xml:id="def-fourier-transform">
  <statement>
    <p>
      The <term>Fourier Transform</term> of a function <m>f(x)</m> denoted <m>\mathcal{F}[f](\omega)</m> or simply <m>\mathcal{F}[f]</m> is
      <md>
        \mathcal{F}[f](\omega) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)e^{i\omega x}~dx.
      </md>
      Note we input a function of <m>x</m> and output a function of <m>\omega</m>. The 
      <term>Inverse Fourier Transform</term> <m>\mathcal{F}^{-1}</m> is
      <md>
        f(x) = \int\limits_{-\infty}^{\infty} \mathcal{F}(\omega)e^{-i\omega x}~d\omega.
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<fact xml:id="fact-fourier-derivative-property">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Differentiation property of the Fourier Transform:</term> for a function <m>f(x)</m>, 
      <md>
        \mathcal{F}[f'] = -i\omega \mathcal{F}[f].
      </md>
      Derivatives in spatial land are simply multiplications in frequency land!
    </p>
  </statement>
</fact>

  <exercise workspace="6in">
    <statement>
      <p>Explain why
  <md>
  \fourier[xf(x)] = -i\frac{d}{d\omega}\fourier[f].
  </md>
  It may be easiest to start with the right hand side and plug in the definition of <m>\mathcal{F}[f]</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-shift-theorem">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>Shift Theorem</term> states that 
        <md>
          \mathcal{F}[f(x-\beta)] = e^{i\omega\beta}\mathcal{F}[f].
        </md>
        This an important theorem for the Fourier Transforms, suggesting you can 
        shift the input variable <m>x</m> by some amount <m>\beta</m> before applying a 
        Fourier Transform, and it results in <m>e^{i\omega\beta}</m> multiplied
        by the Fourier Transform of the unshifted function. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  
  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Explain why the Shift Theorem is true:
  <md>
  \fourier[f(x-\beta)] = e^{i\omega\beta}\fourier[f].
  </md>
  Suggestion: try a change of variable <m>y=x-\beta</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
